IAM User - test-user is created in the console and enabled login console access and MFA for second layer security. 

IAM Group - test-group is created and added the test-user in the group. 

IAM Policy - test-policy will allow EC2 full access and deny terminate instance are created as custom managed policy. Attached custom managed policy to the test-group Now the test-user can access full ec2 instance but deny to terminate the instances. 

IAM Role - 
test-role is created with trust policy as same account and added permission policy as to access full s3 bucket. 
Now, Test-user is authorized to assume the role using inline policy and added the IAM Role ARN in the inline policy. 
Switch to role with temporary credentials, url is copied from the IAM ROLE(test-role). 
URL is copied and pasted on top of test-user account profile. user with test-role can access s3 bucket. 
Note: This can be done like test-user can access the production account from dev-account where the trust policy mention the assume role for production accounts. 
Instead of giving a user account for production, a person can access the production s3 bucket.  (Cross account access)

Least privilege principle covers the test-user not to terminate the instances. 

Resource based policy - where the new lambda function is created in the test-user account and lambda function decides only the Specific API-Gateway to be allowed to access it.

Permission boundary: Even the test-user is given the Admin access & control to all the AWS resources. If we set permission boundary as EC2 only. Then only EC2 can be accessible. 

Access key rotation: Access key rotation is applicable for IAM User and not for EC2 role. AWS automatically do a key rotation with temporary credentials where AWS STS take care. 

Service linked roles: If we create a Autoscalinggroup/ECS/ELB/Lambda. AWS automatically creates a service role. Manually role creation not required. 

IAM Instance Profile: Attach a IAM role to EC2 is not technically correct. IAM Role -> instance Profile -> EC2

Access key VS Secret key: Create a IAM User with access to& secret key. On our local machine install AWS CLI. Then aws configure with access and secret key. Now list s3 bucket. Check IAM User permission to list the buckets or not.
Laptop -> aws s3 ls -> AWS configure -> Access keys -> IAM User -> check identity -> Check permissions -> s3 API



